WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.900
Smart pointers support leak prevention when dynamic ownership is necessary.

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.960
They connect ownership to object lifetime and reduce the need for manual cleanup calls.

3
00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:15.740
They also make ownership easier to see in function signatures, member variables, and factory returns.

4
00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:20.900
std::unique_ptr is usually the default tool for exclusive dynamic ownership.

5
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:27.160
It automatically destroys the object when the owner goes away and makes transfer visible through move semantics.

6
00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:31.260
A unique pointer tells reviewers that one owner is responsible for cleanup.

7
00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:38.900
std::shared_ptr is appropriate when multiple owners genuinely need to keep an object alive.

8
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:43.560
Shared ownership should not be used just because ownership is unclear.

9
00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:52.220
If the design has one natural owner, unique ownership or value ownership is usually simpler and easier to review.

10
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:57.620
std::weak_ptr observes shared objects without extending lifetime.

11
00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:07.160
It can help prevent cycles and support callbacks, registries, or caches that should check whether a target still exists before using it.

12
00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:11.100
Expired weak observations need clear handling.

13
00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:13.780
Smart pointers are not magic.

14
00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:24.280
A program can still leak through shared ownership cycles, long-lived globals, unbounded caches, retained callbacks, or ownership graphs that never release.

15
00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:29.500
Smart pointers work best when they express a clear lifecycle design rather than hiding one.

16
00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:32.740
Function boundaries are a good place to enforce intent.

17
00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:43.240
Returning unique ownership, accepting borrowed references, and storing shared ownership in narrow, reviewed locations helps prevent smart pointers from becoming a substitute for design.
